        "original_id": "efbeb963-96e2-5b7a-b30e-730b25523734",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Robin Hood\n- Products: Lincoln green\n- Places: Lincoln\n- Concepts: Medieval dyeing, folklore depiction\n\nFacts:\n- Robin Hood is traditionally depicted dressed in Lincoln green and is said to rob from the rich and give to the poor.\n- In the high Middle Ages, the dyers of Lincoln produced Lincoln green cloth.\n- The dyers of Lincoln made Lincoln green by first dyeing cloth blue with woad (Isatis tinctoria).\n- The dyers of Lincoln then overdyed the cloth yellow with weld (Reseda luteola) or dyers' broom (Genista tinctoria).",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "7919cd33-ad32-5a22-b64d-fdd19efb1c15",
        "text": "Page: Robin Hood. Sentence: Traditionally depicted dressed in Lincoln green, he is said to rob from the rich and give to the poor.\nPage: Lincoln green. Sentence: The dyers of Lincoln, a cloth town in the high Middle Ages, produced the cloth by dyeing it with woad (\"Isatis tinctoria\") to give it a strong blue, then overdyeing it yellow with weld (\"Reseda luteola\") or dyers' broom, \"Genista tinctoria\". \"",
